# DTF tier manifest: ingest-archive-entries-limit  (artifact-keeper#4093, #4802, 1.11.3)
# Profile-set: filesystem + ingest.archive-entries (passes
# MAX_INGEST_ARCHIVE_ENTRIES to the backend). The cap lives in the upload
# metadata extractors, ahead of storage, so the storage dimension is irrelevant.
#
# THE CHANGE (#4093, fixed by #4802)
# util/bounded_archive.rs MAX_INGEST_ARCHIVE_ENTRIES was a const 10000. Every
# upload path that reads package metadata out of an archive refuses an archive
# with more entries as a suspected entry-count bomb, and there was no way to
# raise it for a legitimate package (the reporter's Composer packages vendor
# their `vendor/` tree). #4802 reads the cap from the MAX_INGEST_ARCHIVE_ENTRIES
# env var (blank / zero / non-numeric fall back to 10000).
#
# Path under test: Composer upload, PUT /composer/<repo>/api/packages, which
# reads composer.json through bounded_archive::read_metadata_from_zip. An
# archive over the cap is refused with
#     400 "Failed to parse composer.json from archive: Validation error: ZIP
#          archive contains too many entries (> N); refusing suspected
#          decompression bomb"
# and no artifact row is written.
#
# Oracle (oracle.sh, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (FIX)     RED on 1.11.2: with the cap set to 50, an 81-entry package is
#             refused 400 naming the cap, and no artifacts row exists.
#   (CONTROL) true on BOTH images: a 21-entry package under the cap is
#             accepted and stored; a second backend started by the oracle from
#             the same image on the same database WITHOUT the variable accepts
#             the same 81-entry package (the default is still 10000).
#
# The value is exported here because run.sh passes only a fixed set of tier
# env vars to compose; the overlay reads it from the environment.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem ingest.archive-entries"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
export MAX_INGEST_ARCHIVE_ENTRIES="50"
